# Copy to .env and fill. NEVER commit .env or real secrets.
#
# That first line is now true. It was not before (#417): every name here lacked the `GV_` prefix the
# application reads, `Settings` never read a `.env` file at all, and the port was 5432 where the local
# stack uses 5433. Following the instruction produced an application that would not start, and nothing
# noticed — so `tests/test_env_example.py` now reads this file and `app/config.py` together, and a
# renamed setting fails the build.
#
# **Only real settings may be uncommented here.** `Settings` sets `extra="forbid"`, so an unknown key in
# `.env` is refused outright — `Settings()` raises rather than ignoring it. That is why the values this
# project will need later but does not read yet are written as comments at the bottom rather than as
# live lines: an active `AWS_REGION` here would stop the API booting.
#
# Precedence, so a stale file cannot surprise you: a real environment variable wins over the same key in
# `.env`. CI and containers pass variables directly, so this file never overrides them.

# The two Claude readers (Opus 5.5 + Sonnet 5.5) are called through OpenRouter by default, on Google
# Vertex with no data kept (#1094); `anthropic` calls Anthropic's own API instead. The keys are
# intentionally not GV_-prefixed settings. Only the chosen route's key is needed, and only when
# GV_CLAUDE_READER_ENABLED=true; set it in your private .env, never in this template or source control.
# GV_CLAUDE_READER_PROVIDER=openrouter
# A new OpenRouter account takes at most 20 calls a minute per model; pace each model below that,
# or the refused calls slow the reading down.
# GV_CLAUDE_READER_MODEL_RPM={"anthropic.claude-opus-5-5": 18, "anthropic.claude-sonnet-5-5": 18}
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# How hard both Claude readers think (low | medium | high | xhigh | max); sent on every call.
# GV_CLAUDE_READER_EFFORT=high
# A re-run reuses a reader's stored answer to the identical question; false asks everything again.
# GV_READER_REUSE_ANSWERS=true

# Bala (#1236): with the slot reader on, the match stage suggests a countertop and its pieces for
# every row the readers read, and every suggested part gets a picture, so both picture settings are
# then required (no defaults). Code's geometry-only part finder checks each piece in memory ($0)
# when switched on; it needs every GV_READER_* line and detector setting the localized reader
# takes, except the three GV_READER_LOCALIZED_* ones, and turns on no other reader.
# GV_PART_PICTURE_MARGIN_PT=72
# GV_PART_PICTURE_DPI=150
# GV_PART_FINDER_ENABLED=1
# Bala's automatic countertops (#1244, ADR-0021 amendment): on, the match stage confirms a countertop
# with no click where both AI row readers named its row AND code finds the row wholly inside one
# drawing confirmed as the vendor's, outlined by its printed overall that its pieces tile. Off by
# default. A person can always undo it on the Measure page, a person's decision always wins, pieces
# stay one click, and no check reads these items until a person confirms the countertop's run.
# Switching it off is NOT a rollback: it stops code making new items, but code's items whose two
# judgments still agree stay; code takes one back only when its judgments stop agreeing, a newer
# reading replaces its row, or a person decides a countertop there. A person removes one with Undo.
# GV_BALA_AUTO_ITEMS=1
# Bala's cabinet pre-fill (#1266): on, a countertop row's piece comes pre-selected as "cabinet" on
# the Measure page where code's part finder reads exactly one cabinet tag printed on it (a part with
# the piece's two ends, one tag inside its span and no other's, and no tag over the row lower than
# it, so a wall cabinet's tag never counts). A pre-fill only: a person still confirms or changes
# it, and nothing is confirmed by code. It needs the part finder (GV_PART_FINDER_ENABLED above, or
# the reader's settings). Off by default; switch it on only when the piece-kind scorecard
# (scripts/pariksha_kinds.py) shows 0 wrong. Only tags starting B, BC, BD, DB or SB (base cabinets)
# count; appliance, filler, panel, wall and tall codes never pre-fill. It governs NEW filings only:
# switching it off does NOT take back pre-fills already filed (they stay one-click pre-selections a
# person must still confirm), and switching it on applies from the next reading of a drawing set.
# GV_BALA_CABINET_PREFILL=1

# What the AI readers may spend on one drawing set (#1206): $0.15 for every page sent to them plus
# $1 for the calls in flight, never below the minimum and never above the maximum (a 16-page set
# gets $3.40, a 60-page set $10). These are the defaults Claude proposed (decision log 2026-10-13);
# the admin may change any of them. Reaching the budget stops the reading cleanly and lists every
# page left unread, which blocks sign-off until it is answered or read again.
# GV_AI_BUDGET_PER_PAGE_USD=0.15
# GV_AI_BUDGET_MARGIN_USD=1.00
# GV_AI_BUDGET_MINIMUM_USD=3.00
# GV_AI_BUDGET_MAXIMUM_USD=25.00
# Optional: a lower ceiling for one Claude reading, at most the maximum above. Unset (the default),
# the page-scaled budget alone applies. It replaced the old fixed $2.50 (at most $5).
# GV_CLAUDE_READER_BUDGET_USD=
# Optional, and read from the worker's own environment, not from this file: a fixed amount for one
# drawing set in place of the page-scaled budget (a proof run). It replaced the old fixed $3.
# GV_AI_BUDGET_PER_SET_USD=

# The review assistant (#1128): Claude Sonnet 5.5 through OpenRouter on the same keep-no-data route,
# answering only from a review's records. Off unless enabled and OPENROUTER_API_KEY (above) is set.
# Each call's cost is recorded for the Usage page (priced from GV_MODEL_RATES_FILE when set).
# GV_REVIEW_ASSISTANT_ENABLED=
# GV_REVIEW_ASSISTANT_MODEL=
# GV_REVIEW_ASSISTANT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=
# GV_REVIEW_ASSISTANT_MAX_HISTORY_TURNS=

# The Usage page can show what OpenRouter reports this key has used, across every project using the
# key (#1165): a free, read-only request with OPENROUTER_API_KEY (above). Off unless set to true.
# GV_USAGE_PROVIDER_CHECK=false

# --- Required ---

# The application database. Port 5433, matching docker-compose.yml — deliberately not 5432, so it
# cannot collide with a PostgreSQL you already run and connect you to the wrong database in silence.
GV_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://gv:gv@localhost:5433/gv

# The engine client token. Not strictly required by `Settings` — it defaults to empty — but a worker
# refuses to start without it, so leaving it out gives you a process that exits 78 and a puzzle.
#
# Where the value comes from: run `make up`, then `make token` (#416). It prints the line to paste here
# and also writes `.hatchet-token`, which is gitignored. Tokens expire after 90 days; run it again.
GV_HATCHET_TOKEN=

# --- Optional: uncomment to override the default ---

# Which environment this is. It affects what /health reports — and it is also one of the two locks on
# the development identity below, which `app/auth/development.py` installs only when this says
# `development`. The default is already `development`, so a laptop needs this only to be explicit.
#GV_ENVIRONMENT=development

# The request-id header the API accepts and echoes.
#GV_REQUEST_ID_HEADER=X-Request-ID

# Namespace prefix for workflow names, so two environments can share one engine without one picking up
# the other's packages. Empty means no prefix.
#GV_HATCHET_NAMESPACE=

# How many package revisions may be processed at once. 1 on purpose: one 8 GB VM shares memory between
# rendering, OCR and PostgreSQL. Raising it should follow a measurement, not an assumption.
#GV_MAX_CONCURRENT_PACKAGES=1

# How many tasks one worker runs at once.
#GV_MAX_CONCURRENT_PAGE_TASKS=2

# How long the outbox dispatcher waits between polls. This is the visible wait between a package being
# accepted and its workflow starting, and somebody is watching.
#GV_OUTBOX_POLL_SECONDS=2.0

# How many outbox rows one pass may dispatch. Bounds both the transaction's lock time and how much work
# repeats if the process dies mid-pass.
#GV_OUTBOX_BATCH_LIMIT=100

# How far, in stored page units, a part may stray past a countertop's ends and top and still be
# suggested as part of the run beneath it (#893), and how far a reading's ends may lie from a part's
# and the reading still be suggested as its width (#913). There is no default: unset, the Measure
# page suggests no run and no reading, and no run can be confirmed. `scripts/demo.sh` states the
# reader's witness tolerance.
#GV_RUN_EDGE_TOLERANCE=

# The architect's numbers on a combined sheet, read by code from the text inside the pasted drawing
# (#1052): no AI, no cost. Off by default. When on, a drawing whose exact heading and own content
# agree on its role is confirmed by code (a person's confirmation always wins), and the architect's
# values are stored as candidates. Read by the worker (`workflow/entrypoints.py`) and the local worker
# (`scripts/drain_outbox.py`).
#GV_ARCHITECT_READER_ENABLED=false

# --- Read from the environment, and NOT from this file ---
#
# **Putting these in `.env` stops the application starting.** They are read by
# `app/auth/development.py` through `os.environ` directly, so they are not fields on `Settings` — and
# `extra="forbid"` means `Settings()` refuses an unknown key it finds in `.env`. Verified, not
# assumed: with both written into `.env`, `Settings()` raises `extra_forbidden` for each and the API
# never boots. `tests/test_env_example.py` would also fail the build if they were live keys here,
# because it checks every active key against a real setting.
#
# So export them for the process instead:
#
#   GV_DEV_PRINCIPAL="your name" \
#   GV_DEV_PROJECTS=<project-uuid> \
#   python scripts/dev_server.py
#
# `GV_DEV_PRINCIPAL` is the signed-in person's name; setting it at all is what asks for a development
# identity. `GV_DEV_PROJECTS` is a comma-separated list of project UUIDs that identity may see — the
# API authorises per project, so a principal with the wrong list gets 403 on findings that exist.
# `scripts/seed_demo.py` prints the project id it created, which is the one to use.
#
# Both are refused unless `GV_ENVIRONMENT` says `development`. Two locks rather than one: a deployment
# that forgets the environment still has to have exported the variable, and one that exports the
# variable still has to be calling itself development. No credential is checked, which is why.

# --- Not read by the application yet ---
#
# Kept as comments, not as live keys, for the reason at the top: `extra="forbid"` means an unknown key in
# `.env` stops the application starting. These become real settings when the stories that need them land,
# and each will get a field on `Settings` at that point.
#
# Object storage (C5 — S3 backend, #221, currently deferred):
#   AWS_REGION=us-east-1
#   S3_BUCKET=gv-v1-artifacts
#
# Bedrock (E2 — the VLM lane, ambiguous crops only). The verdict service must NOT receive these:
#   BEDROCK_MODEL_ID=amazon.nova-2-lite
#
# Traces (F2.1, #259). The tracer is built but no exporter is configured — where traces are shipped is an
# operations decision that has not been made:
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
#
# The engine's own database. Not an application setting: docker-compose.yml passes it to the engine
# container directly, and the engine — not Alembic — owns that schema:
#   HATCHET_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://gv:gv@localhost:5433/hatchet
